1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,578
Hallo. This is Liam, in for Bear. Claude just opened a portal for plugins. So the

2
00:00:04,598 --> 00:00:08,314
question isn't only how you build one. It's how you get one live.

3
00:00:08,384 --> 00:00:12,980
Three terms. MCP, the Model Context Protocol: an open standard that plugs Claude

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,950
into outside tools and data. A skill: written instructions Claude loads to do one

5
00:00:17,970 --> 00:00:23,276
kind of task well. And a plugin: a package that carries MCP connectors, skills, or

6
00:00:23,296 --> 00:00:23,631
both.

7
00:00:23,701 --> 00:00:28,410
Start with the plugin itself. Picture it as a sealed box. What goes inside?

8
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:33,323
A plugin is a package. Inside go MCP connectors, which plug Claude into outside

9
00:00:33,343 --> 00:00:37,839
tools and data, and skills, which are written instructions Claude can follow. A

10
00:00:37,859 --> 00:00:40,271
plugin can carry either one, or both.

11
00:00:40,341 --> 00:00:46,147
There are two ways to submit one. Way one is a single MCP connector. You point it at

12
00:00:46,167 --> 00:00:49,060
your own remote MCP server, and that's the submission.

13
00:00:49,130 --> 00:00:54,787
Way two is a plugin bundle. You put your MCP servers and your skills together in a

14
00:00:54,807 --> 00:00:57,124
GitHub repository, and you submit the repo.

15
00:00:57,194 --> 00:01:01,421
You submit right inside Claude, from the directory's manage page, if you're a

16
00:01:01,441 --> 00:01:06,321
developer on a paid plan. The moment you hit submit, the plugin is checked and

17
00:01:06,341 --> 00:01:08,281
safety-scanned, so problems show up early.

18
00:01:08,351 --> 00:01:13,252
Then you can track it. The portal shows where your plugin is in review, what the

19
00:01:13,272 --> 00:01:16,942
safety scan found, and any changes it recommends. Submitted. In review. Live.

20
00:01:17,012 --> 00:01:21,598
Once it's live, you can see it being used: installs by product and version, how

21
00:01:21,618 --> 00:01:25,283
often your listing gets viewed, and which searches lead people to it.

22
00:01:25,353 --> 00:01:29,311
Why does this matter now? Claude's developer team says MCP usage across Claude

23
00:01:29,331 --> 00:01:34,206
products is up a hundred and ten times this year, and that plugins are becoming the

24
00:01:34,226 --> 00:01:35,736
way to build for Claude.

25
00:01:35,806 --> 00:01:40,285
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: I use [name a tool] every week. Help me plan a

26
00:01:40,305 --> 00:01:44,520
Claude plugin for it: one MCP connector that exposes only the two or three actions I

27
00:01:44,540 --> 00:01:49,020
actually need, and one skill that says when and how to use them well. List what the

28
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:52,725
connector needs access to, and flag anything it should not expose. Don't write code

29
00:01:52,745 --> 00:01:56,166
yet. Then check two things yourself. Does every action trace to something you

30
00:01:56,186 --> 00:01:59,871
actually do with that tool? And does the connector reach anything the job doesn't

31
00:01:59,891 --> 00:02:02,253
need? If it does, cut it before you submit.

32
00:02:02,323 --> 00:02:07,370
Claude Plugins: Package, Submit, Go Live. At Nik Bear Brown.
